"_score_p_tbv":0.0,"_gate_mult_vs_hist":0.16055401192330332,"_gp_mult_vs_hist":false,"_score_mult_vs_hist":56.0,"_gate_fcf_yield":0.02515201086457663,"_gp_fcf_yield":false,"_score_fcf_yield":5.2,"_gate_int_coverage":13.952380952380953,"_gp_int_coverage":true,"_score_int_coverage":68.2,"_gate_net_debt_ebitda":-0.6802721088435374,"_gp_net_debt_ebitda":true,"_score_net_debt_ebitda":100.0,"_gate_accruals":-0.01961658493089612,"_gp_accruals":true,"_score_accruals":28.3,"_gate_rev_volatility":0.08262938204364546,"_gp_rev_volatility":true,"_score_rev_volatility":90.7,"_gate_margin_vs_hist":-0.016210534794413436,"_gp_margin_vs_hist":true,"_score_margin_vs_hist":100.0,"_gate_piotroski":7,"_gp_piotroski":true,"_score_piotroski":77.8,"_gate_spread":0.014833025328504182,"_gp_spread":false,"_score_spread":7.4,"_gate_roic_consistency":0.4089370604472575,"_gp_roic_consistency":false,"_score_roic_consistency":31.8,"_gate_incr_roic":0.1552756892230576,"_gp_incr_roic":true,"_score_incr_roic":68.4,"_gate_margin_advantage":0.005019769267528576,"_gp_margin_advantage":false,"_score_margin_advantage":35.0,"_gate_pool_share":0.03887105988991402,"_gp_pool_share":true,"_score_pool_share":55.5,"_gate_rev_durability":null,"_gp_rev_durability":null,"_score_rev_durability":0.0,"_gate_fcf_durability":null,"_gp_fcf_durability":null,"_score_fcf_durability":0.0,"_gate_margins":0.011049780299837764,"_gp_margins":true,"_score_margins":61.0,"_gate_fund_growth":0.03397829479040302,"_gp_fund_growth":true,"_score_fund_growth":34.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.013516002625864615,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":false,"_score_shrhldr_yield":26.1,"_gate_share_shrink":0.004929415136097903,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":43.8,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.008771929824561403,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":85.4,"_gate_insider_own":0.71469,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":100.0,"_gate_insider_buying":0.0,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":0.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"Leonardo DRS, Inc., together with its subsidiaries, provides defense electronic products and systems, and military support services worldwide. It operates through Advanced Sensing and Computing and Integrated Mission Systems segments. The Advanced Sensing and Computing segment designs, develops, and manufacture sensing and network computing technology that enables real-time situational awareness required for enhanced operational decision making and execution; and offers sensing capabilities span applications, such as missions requiring advanced detection, precision targeting and surveillance sensing, long range electro-optic/infrared, signals intelligence, and other intelligence systems including electronic warfare, ground vehicle sensing, active electronically scanned array tactical radars, dismounted soldier, and space sensing. This segment also provides network computing, which are utilized across a range of mission applications, such as platform computing on ground and shipboard for advanced battle management, combat systems, radar, command and control, tactical networks, tactical computing, and communications. The Integrated Mission Systems segment offers electrical propulsion systems, which includes power conversion, control, distribution, and propulsion systems, as well as power dense permanent magnet motors, energy storage systems, associated rugged and compact power conversion, electrical actuation systems, as well as advanced thermal management technologies, motor controllers, and instrumentation and control equipment. Leonardo DRS, Inc. was incorporated in 1968 and is based in Arlington, Virginia. Leonardo DRS, Inc. operates as a subsidiary of Leonardo US Holding, LLC.","ceo_bio":"Mr. John A. Baylouny | President, CEO & Director | Age 64 | Compensation: $1.4M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 7K people. Revenue per employee of $500K ranks in the above the sector median among Industrials peers, with revenue growing 6%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $31K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $4K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 3\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["Leonardo DRS, Inc. is a mid-cap industrials business with a $9.0B market cap on $3.6B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +11% over the past three years, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 10% against a 9% cost of capital, which means the business barely earns its keep economically, and 23% gross margins are typical of commodity economics where competitors can replicate the product cheaply.","The business throws off $227M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 6 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.32\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and interest coverage of 14\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield is only 1.4%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 71% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 76% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.759350723493391},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $4.5M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Cyclicality and capital intensity \u2014 order books turn on a dime, and the heavy fixed cost base that creates the moat also magnifies the downturn.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Supply chain reshoring costs and labour shortages \u2014 onshoring mandates raise capex requirements while a shrinking skilled-labour pool inflates operating costs.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Tariff and trade-policy exposure \u2014 industrial exporters sit squarely in the crosshairs of protectionist policy, and retaliatory tariffs can close markets without warning.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading near the bottom of its 52-week range \u2014 the market is offering a discount","Piotroski score 7/9 \u2014 financials improving across profitability, leverage and efficiency","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Can Leonardo DRS (NASDAQ:DRS) Turn Its New Contract Into Growth?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/industrial/can-leonardo-drs-nasdaqdrs-turn-its-new-contract-into-growth","date":"2026-11-24","timestamp":1795544760.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.402,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["product"]},{"title":"Leonardo DRS, Inc. (DRS) Forms 'Hammer Chart Pattern': Time for Bottom Fishing?","source":"zacks.com","link":"https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3034535/leonardo-drs-inc-drs-forms-hammer-chart-pattern-time-for-bottom-fishing?cid=CS-ZC-FT-fundamental_analysis%7Chammer_chart_pattern_%28ta%29-3034535","date":"2026-11-24","timestamp":1795528503.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Behavioral Patterns of DRS and Institutional Flows","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/16/Behavioral_Patterns_of_DRS_and_Institutional_Flows_112026065201_1795218721.html","date":"2026-11-20","timestamp":1795218721.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":